{"id":"ltc-related-pay-while-losing","title":"Homes reporting a loss while paying related companies more than the loss","question":"Which nursing homes report losing money in a year in which they paid companies under common ownership more than the amount lost?","finding":"In 2025, 119 California nursing homes (21 in the Bay Area) reported a combined loss of $69.8 million while listing $232.0 million of payments to related parties; across the five report years, 481 homes did so at least once. In San Francisco it happened 6 times: City View Post Acute 2022 (loss $4.3 million, paid related parties $5.3 million); City View Post Acute 2023 (loss $1.6 million, paid related parties $3.2 million); Lawton Skilled Nursing and Rehabilitation Center 2019 (loss $312,852, paid related parties $1.5 million); San Francisco Health Care & Rehab 2024 (loss $653,754, paid related parties $1.8 million); The Sequoias San Francisco 2025 (loss $148,430, paid related parties $3.4 million); Tunnell Skilled Nursing and Rehabilitation Center 2019 (loss $1.6 million, paid related parties $2.8 million).","how":"Net income from page 8 line 255 of each report; payments to related parties as in the screen above. A home-year is listed when net income is below zero, related-party payments are at least $500,000 and at least as large as the loss. Home-years from the five report years on file (2019, 2022, 2023, 2024, 2025).","limits":"A loss at the home while the owner's other companies are paid is the pattern lenders and regulators look at when profit may be moved out of a home, but it has ordinary causes: rent set years earlier, a bad year for occupancy, a new owner's start-up costs, or a non-profit campus whose costs are shared out by its central office. The file does not show whether any payment was above a fair price. Pay to individuals is counted, never named.","tier":"REDFLAG","columns":[{"k":"home","t":"Nursing home","type":"org"},{"k":"city","t":"City","type":"text"},{"k":"county","t":"County",